  "title": "Say, Speak and Call: dict, loqu, voc, nounce and mand",
  "short": "dict means say, loqu means speak, voc means call, nounce means announce and mand means order.",
  "trouble": "dict hides in verdict (ver + dict: a true saying) and indict, said in-DITE.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.word-parts"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Speech gave Latin many roots, and English kept them all. <b>dict</b> means “say”: to <i>predict</i> is to say before, to <i>contradict</i> is to say against. <b>loqu</b> (also <b>locut</b>) means “speak”: a <i>ventriloquist</i> seems to speak from the belly. <b>voc</b> means “call” or “voice” (<i>vocal</i>, <i>invoke</i>). <b>nounce</b> means “announce” or “report” (<i>pronounce</i>), and <b>mand</b> means “order” (<i>command</i>, <i>demand</i>)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
